# =============================================================================
# molaro_T-20_h1.00_22h_a5.62e-3.opts
#
# Rung of the alpha_c LADDER on the tangent Molaro geometry. Pair with
# geometry/molaro/molaro_2D_L387um_eps0.24um_axisym_T-20pair_tangent.opts.
#
# WHAT THE LADDER TESTS
# ---------------------
# alpha_c sets which transport regime the run is in, via the crossover length
# L* = beta_HK*D_v: below it attachment kinetics limit the neck flux, above it
# vapour diffusion does. Kuczynski's r ~ t^(1/3) is DERIVED for the
# attachment-limited case, so this ladder asks the sharpest question we have:
# does the growth exponent turn on as the run crosses into that regime?
#
#   alpha_c    beta_eff/beta(ice)   r_neck/L*   regime
#   1.341e-2        0.727             1.93      mixed      (the 15h file)
#   1.000e-2        0.797             1.44      mixed
#   5.620e-3        0.886             0.81      attachment
#   1.000e-3        0.980             0.14      attachment
#
# All four rungs share ONE geometry. eps = 2.4002e-07 is fixed by the
# neck-resolution floor (r/R >= sqrt(12*eps/R)), which does not depend on
# alpha_c, so the mesh 2281x713 is valid for every rung and alpha_c is the
# only thing that varies. That is what makes this a clean test rather than a
# confound.
#
# WHY THESE alpha_c VALUES
# ------------------------
# Braun, Fourteau & Lowe, The Cryosphere 18(4) 1653-1668 (2024),
# doi:10.5194/tc-18-1653-2024, report best agreement for 1e-3 < alpha < 1e-1,
# with optimal fits 1e-1, 1e-1.5 and 1e-2.25 = 5.62e-3. This ladder walks the
# BOTTOM decade of that range, because the top of it is unusable here: at
# alpha_c = 1e-1 the Eq. 42a ceiling forces ~1300M nodes and beta_eff/beta
# goes to -1.95, i.e. the effective kinetic coefficient changes sign.
#
# Caveats worth carrying into the write-up: Braun's range comes from FITTING
# SSA evolution under a temperature GRADIENT, not from measuring alpha, and
# they call it an effective coefficient. It is also not a function of
# temperature -- they say alpha depends on T, supersaturation and
# crystallographic orientation, then hold it constant. Our runs are isothermal
# (grad_temp0 = 0), so their series-1 two-stage behaviour should not appear
# here; their single-valued series 2 (5.62e-3) is the closer analogue.
#
# THIS RUNG: alpha_c = 5.620e-03  ->  mixed-limited, beta_eff/beta = 0.886
#
# beta_sub0 = beta_HK/(rho_vs/rho_ice) at this alpha_c; d0_sub0 is
# alpha-independent. dtmax = min(1.1*tau_sub, t_final/300) -- the 300-step
# floor matters because tau_sub grows as 1/alpha_c and the campaign rule alone
# would leave the low rungs with ~90 steps, too few to read a slope from.
#
# t_final scales with the total resistance beta_sub + r/(D_v*chi), calibrated
# against the 15 h file at alpha_c = 1.341e-2. Slower kinetics need longer
# runs, but bigger timesteps come with them, so the step count FALLS down the
# ladder (300 steps here) and every rung is ~60 ranks on 1.63M nodes.
#
# Measure: python postprocess/neck_width.py <run_dir> --axisym
# Fit:     python postprocess/fit_neck_growth.py <run>/neck_width.csv \
#              inputs/validation/molaro2019_fig11_T-20.csv \
#              --anchor-neck 1.6405e-5 --demmenie
# =============================================================================

-t_final 7.957e+04

-temp -20.0
-humidity 1.00
-grad_temp0 0.0,0.0,0.0

-beta_sub0 1.4130e+06                     # K&P beta0 at -20 C, alpha_c = 5.620e-03
-d0_sub0 1.0166e-9                      # physical capillary length at -20 C

-dtmax 2.652e+02
-dtCFL 1
-dtCFL_dphimax 0.2

-t_out_log 80
-t_out_log_t0 1.0e2
